Let $\alpha=\arccos\left(-\frac{8}{17}\right)$, so $\cos\alpha=-\frac{8}{17}$.
Since $0\leq\alpha\leq\pi$ and $\cos\alpha<0$, $\alpha$ is in quadrant 2.
The reference triangle has adjacent side $8$ and hypotenuse $17$.
$\begin{align*}o&=\sqrt{17^2-8^2}\\&=15\end{align*}$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0)--(1.6,0)--(0,3)--cycle;
\draw (0.18,0)--(0.18,0.18)--(0,0.18);
\path (0.8,0) node[below] {$8$};
\path (0,1.5) node[left] {$15$};
\path (0.8,1.5) node[above right] {$17$};
\draw (1.15,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=118.07,radius=0.45];
\path (0.95,0.4) node[above left] {$\pi-\alpha$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Sine is positive in quadrant 2, so $\sin\alpha=\frac{15}{17}$.
$\begin{align*}\sin 2\alpha&=2\sin\alpha\cos\alpha\\&=2\times\frac{15}{17}\times\left(-\frac{8}{17}\right)\\&=-\frac{240}{289}\end{align*}$

Let $\alpha=\arccos\left(-\frac{3}{5}\right)$, so $\cos\alpha=-\frac{3}{5}$.
Since $0\leq\alpha\leq\pi$ and $\cos\alpha<0$, $\alpha$ is in quadrant 2.
$\begin{align*}o&=\sqrt{5^2-3^2}\\&=4\end{align*}$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0)--(2.1,0)--(0,2.8)--cycle;
\draw (0.18,0)--(0.18,0.18)--(0,0.18);
\path (1.05,0) node[below] {$3$};
\path (0,1.4) node[left] {$4$};
\path (1.05,1.4) node[above right] {$5$};
\draw (1.7,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=126.87,radius=0.4];
\path (1.28,0.35) node {$\pi-\alpha$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Tangent is negative in quadrant 2, so $\tan\alpha=-\frac{4}{3}$.
$\begin{align*}\tan 2\alpha&=\frac{2\tan\alpha}{1-\tan^2\alpha}\\&=\frac{2\left(-\frac{4}{3}\right)}{1-\frac{16}{9}}\\&=\frac{-\frac{8}{3}}{-\frac{7}{9}}\\&=\frac{24}{7}\end{align*}$

Let $\alpha=\arctan\left(-\frac{3}{4}\right)$ and $\beta=\arccos\left(-\frac{12}{13}\right)$.
The range of arctangent places $\alpha$ in quadrant 4; the range of arccosine places $\beta$ in quadrant 2.
For $\alpha$, use a reference triangle with legs $3$ and $4$. For $\beta$, use adjacent side $12$ and hypotenuse $13$.
$\begin{align*}h_\alpha&=\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5\\o_\beta&=\sqrt{13^2-12^2}=5\end{align*}$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0)--(2.4,0)--(0,1.8)--cycle;
\draw (0.16,0)--(0.16,0.16)--(0,0.16);
\path (1.2,0) node[below] {$4$};
\path (0,0.9) node[left] {$3$};
\path (1.2,0.9) node[above right] {$5$};
\draw (2,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=143.13,radius=0.4];
\path (1.65,0.24) node {$-\alpha$};
\draw (4,0)--(7,0)--(4,1.25)--cycle;
\draw (4.16,0)--(4.16,0.16)--(4,0.16);
\path (5.5,0) node[below] {$12$};
\path (4,0.625) node[left] {$5$};
\path (5.5,0.625) node[above right] {$13$};
\draw (6.5,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=157.38,radius=0.5];
\path (5.95,0.19) node {$\pi-\beta$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Apply the quadrant signs: $\sin\alpha=-\frac{3}{5}$, $\cos\alpha=\frac{4}{5}$, $\sin\beta=\frac{5}{13}$ and $\cos\beta=-\frac{12}{13}$.
$\begin{align*}\sin(\alpha+\beta)&=\sin\alpha\cos\beta+\cos\alpha\sin\beta\\&=\left(-\frac{3}{5}\right)\left(-\frac{12}{13}\right)+\frac{4}{5}\times\frac{5}{13}\\&=\frac{36}{65}+\frac{20}{65}\\&=\frac{56}{65}\end{align*}$
For a difference, use $\sin(\alpha-\beta)=\sin\alpha\cos\beta-\cos\alpha\sin\beta$.
